1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,458
A foreign key does not only check new child rows.

2
00:00:03,458 --> 00:00:07,430
It also decides what happens when the parent moves or leaves.

3
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:13,611
ON DELETE RESTRICT or NO ACTION refuses to delete a parent that still has children.

4
00:00:13,611 --> 00:00:18,638
That is the safe default in spirit, even when the dialect wording differs.

5
00:00:18,638 --> 00:00:21,513
Maya still has orders, so Maya stays.

6
00:00:21,513 --> 00:00:24,597
ON DELETE CASCADE deletes the children too.

7
00:00:24,597 --> 00:00:27,819
Delete the order, and the order lines go with it.

8
00:00:27,819 --> 00:00:29,680
That matches a weak entity.

9
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:31,472
It is violent on a customer.

10
00:00:31,472 --> 00:00:36,402
Do not cascade a customer delete unless you truly mean to erase history.

11
00:00:36,402 --> 00:00:40,388
ON DELETE SET NULL clears the pointer and keeps the child.

12
00:00:40,388 --> 00:00:43,069
The order remains, with no customer.

13
00:00:43,069 --> 00:00:44,944
The column must allow NULL.

14
00:00:44,944 --> 00:00:48,916
ON DELETE SET DEFAULT writes the default value instead.

15
00:00:48,916 --> 00:00:53,013
Useful when a placeholder parent exists, rare otherwise.

16
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:57,666
ON UPDATE CASCADE copies a changed parent key into the children.

17
00:00:57,666 --> 00:01:01,166
If you never change primary keys, you barely need it.

18
00:01:01,166 --> 00:01:04,583
That is one more reason to prefer dull surrogates.

19
00:01:04,583 --> 00:01:08,319
Write the action next to the foreign key, not in a wiki.

20
00:01:08,319 --> 00:01:10,527
The engine will not read the wiki.

21
00:01:10,527 --> 00:01:13,791
Different relationships deserve different actions.

22
00:01:13,791 --> 00:01:16,777
order_lines cascade from orders.

23
00:01:16,777 --> 00:01:19,041
orders restrict from customers.

24
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:23,305
credentials cascade from customers if you treat them as a part.

25
00:01:23,305 --> 00:01:24,833
Think in sentences.

26
00:01:24,833 --> 00:01:29,000
When an order disappears, its lines were never independent.

27
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,569
When a customer disappears, the orders are history, so refuse, or archive, or soft-delete instead.

28
00:01:36,569 --> 00:01:41,222
Soft-delete is an application pattern: a deleted_at column.

29
00:01:41,222 --> 00:01:43,208
It is not an ON DELETE action.

30
00:01:43,208 --> 00:01:45,236
Do not mix them without a plan.

31
00:01:45,236 --> 00:01:47,486
Test the action with two rows.

32
00:01:47,486 --> 00:01:48,819
Delete the parent.

33
00:01:48,819 --> 00:01:52,555
See whether the child vanished, blocked, or went null.

34
00:01:52,555 --> 00:01:54,236
Then keep that behavior.

35
00:01:54,236 --> 00:01:58,666
ON DELETE CASCADE on a tall tree can remove more than you see.

36
00:01:58,666 --> 00:02:05,416
A customer cascade that also cascades orders that cascade lines that cascade shipments is a cliff.

37
00:02:05,416 --> 00:02:07,750
Draw the cliff before you enable it.

38
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:11,569
RESTRICT two levels up even if the bottom cascades.

39
00:02:11,569 --> 00:02:14,444
NO ACTION and RESTRICT are close.

40
00:02:14,444 --> 00:02:19,305
The difference is when the check runs, especially with deferred constraints.

41
00:02:19,305 --> 00:02:21,833
In classroom SQL they feel the same.

42
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:24,347
In a long transaction they might not.

43
00:02:24,347 --> 00:02:27,375
SET NULL can surprise a NOT NULL you forgot.

44
00:02:27,375 --> 00:02:31,013
The ALTER will fail, or the delete will fail later.

45
00:02:31,013 --> 00:02:32,444
Pair them on purpose.

46
00:02:32,444 --> 00:02:36,055
Some teams never cascade from an application user.

47
00:02:36,055 --> 00:02:42,222
They delete children in a script, then delete the parent, all inside one transaction.

48
00:02:42,222 --> 00:02:46,680
The foreign key still RESTRICT, which catches a missed child table.

49
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:48,388
That is a valid policy.

50
00:02:48,388 --> 00:02:50,638
The engine is the last reviewer.

51
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:54,347
Triggers that delete children are a homemade cascade.

52
00:02:54,347 --> 00:02:58,111
Prefer the declared action unless you need extra logging.

53
00:02:58,111 --> 00:03:01,763
Logging can live in a trigger that does not replace the action.

54
00:03:01,763 --> 00:03:03,263
Document the cliff.

55
00:03:03,263 --> 00:03:04,666
Test it on a copy.

56
00:03:04,666 --> 00:03:07,097
Then the production delete is boring.

57
00:03:07,097 --> 00:03:08,847
Boring deletes are the goal.

58
00:03:08,847 --> 00:03:14,958
Actions are how you choose the boredom: refuse, take the children, or drop the pointer.

59
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:16,138
Pick per arrow.

60
00:03:16,138 --> 00:03:17,805
Write it in the constraint.

61
00:03:17,805 --> 00:03:18,625
Sleep.

62
00:03:18,625 --> 00:03:21,263
An action is a story about grief.

63
00:03:21,263 --> 00:03:22,638
The parent is gone.

64
00:03:22,638 --> 00:03:26,875
Do the children vanish, stay unnamed, or block the funeral?

65
00:03:26,875 --> 00:03:28,638
Pick the story per arrow.

66
00:03:28,638 --> 00:03:31,250
Do not inherit CASCADE from a snippet.

67
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:34,097
Snippets do not know your history table.

68
00:03:34,097 --> 00:03:35,555
Restrict history.

69
00:03:35,555 --> 00:03:36,930
Cascade parts.

70
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:40,027
Null only when unnamed is a real state.

71
00:03:40,027 --> 00:03:43,041
Test with two rows, not with production.

72
00:03:43,041 --> 00:03:45,597
Read the row counts after the delete.

73
00:03:45,597 --> 00:03:48,416
Keep the story that matches those counts.

74
00:03:48,416 --> 00:03:50,708
Write the story in the constraint.

75
00:03:50,708 --> 00:03:53,027
Then a new engineer will not guess.

76
00:03:53,027 --> 00:03:55,361
Guessing is how cliffs get steeper.

77
00:03:55,361 --> 00:03:57,708
Declared actions are the railing.

78
00:03:57,708 --> 00:04:03,694
A foreign key is a link: one or more columns in a child that reference a parent's key.

79
00:04:03,694 --> 00:04:07,486
A review points at a product and may also point at a user.

80
00:04:07,486 --> 00:04:10,472
Two foreign keys on one child is normal.

81
00:04:10,472 --> 00:04:12,180
Name each constraint.

82
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:23,722
CONSTRAINT reviews_product_fk FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES products (product_id) is a sentence the engine can run.

83
00:04:23,722 --> 00:04:32,777
The default referential action is NO ACTION or RESTRICT, depending on dialect: refuse the parent change if children exist.

84
00:04:32,777 --> 00:04:38,263
That default is how you do not evaporate history by deleting a product someone reviewed.

85
00:04:38,378 --> 00:04:42,855
ON DELETE CASCADE deletes matching children when the parent goes.

86
00:04:42,855 --> 00:04:52,411
Use it when the child cannot exist without the parent and you mean to destroy the whole bundle: order lines when an unfinished order is discarded.

87
00:04:52,411 --> 00:04:55,994
Do not cascade a product into a year of invoices.

88
00:04:55,994 --> 00:05:00,703
ON DELETE SET NULL clears the child's pointer and keeps the child row.

89
00:05:00,703 --> 00:05:06,716
Reviews might remain as anonymous text if a user is removed and the business allows it.

90
00:05:06,716 --> 00:05:08,591
The column must allow NULL.

91
00:05:08,591 --> 00:05:15,064
ON DELETE SET DEFAULT points the child at a declared default parent, such as a placeholder user.

92
00:05:15,064 --> 00:05:20,203
ON UPDATE CASCADE rewrites the copied key if the parent's key changes.

93
00:05:20,203 --> 00:05:25,508
If your primary keys are dull and never edited, you will barely need ON UPDATE.

94
00:05:25,508 --> 00:05:27,966
That is a reason dull keys are kind.

95
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:34,466
If you used a natural key people edit, ON UPDATE CASCADE is a moving truck for every child.

96
00:05:34,466 --> 00:05:36,828
Prefer not to move the house numbers.

97
00:05:36,828 --> 00:05:39,591
Prefer a surrogate that never changes.

98
00:05:39,591 --> 00:05:50,064
Match the action to the sentence in requirements: when a parent leaves, children vanish, children stay with a hole, or the parent is not allowed to leave.

99
00:05:50,064 --> 00:05:52,369
Write that sentence in the constraint.

100
00:05:52,369 --> 00:05:58,341
Test it: insert parent, insert child, delete parent, SELECT the child.

101
00:05:58,341 --> 00:06:03,244
If the child is gone and you wanted a hole, you cascaded by mistake.

102
00:06:03,244 --> 00:06:08,341
If the delete errors and you wanted a bundle gone, you restricted by mistake.

103
00:06:08,341 --> 00:06:11,411
Multiple children mean multiple policies.

104
00:06:11,411 --> 00:06:13,064
Payments might restrict.

105
00:06:13,064 --> 00:06:14,966
Draft lines might cascade.

106
00:06:14,966 --> 00:06:18,758
Do not pick one action for the whole database out of fashion.

107
00:06:18,758 --> 00:06:20,328
Pick per relationship.

108
00:06:20,328 --> 00:06:25,286
SQLite still needs foreign keys enabled or none of the actions run.

109
00:06:25,286 --> 00:06:29,564
Turn them on in every connection that should obey the design.

110
00:06:29,564 --> 00:06:35,494
Then delete and update are part of the schema, not a scavenger hunt in application code.

